T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze the historic 'War of the Currents' between Thomas Edison's DC system and George Westinghouse & Nikola Tesla's AC system.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   The War of the Currents \(1880s - 1890s\):) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. Edison's Direct Current \(DC\) System:) Tj
T*
(   Thomas Edison established the Pearl Street Station in New York City \(1882\) supplying) Tj
T*
(   low-voltage DC \(110 V\) to incandescent lamps. However, transmitting power at 110 V required) Tj
T*
(   enormous conductor cross-sections because line losses are proportional to current squared) Tj
T*
(   \($P_{loss} = I^2 R$\). Transmitting high power required heavy currents \($I = P/V$\).) Tj
T*
(   Consequently, Edison's generating stations had a transmission radius capped at roughly 1 to 2) Tj
T*
(   kilometers, necessitating a separate generating station in every neighborhood.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. The Westinghouse-Tesla Alternating Current \(AC\) Paradigm:) Tj
T*
(   Nikola Tesla patented the polyphase AC induction motor and generation system in 1888.) Tj
T*
(   Partnering with George Westinghouse, they utilized Lucien Gaulard and John Dixon Gibbs') Tj
T*
(   transformer concept, perfected by William Stanley. The AC transformer allowed voltage to be) Tj
T*
(   stepped up to thousands of volts \($V_{high}$\) for long-distance transmission, slashing current) Tj
T*
(   \($I = P / V_{high}$\) and reducing $I^2 R$ losses by a factor of $\(V_{high} / V_{low}\)^2$.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Climax at the 1893 Chicago World's Fair and Niagara Falls \(1895\):) Tj
T*
(   Westinghouse underbid General Electric to illuminate the 1893 World's Columbian Exposition) Tj
T*
