(   [Problem Statement]: Describe the operating principles and protection zones of Distance Relaying for transmission lines.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Distance Protection Principles:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Distance relays measure the ratio of voltage to current at the relay location to calculate the) Tj
T*
(   impedance $Z = V / I$ of the transmission line. Since line impedance is directly proportional) Tj
T*
(   to physical distance, the relay determines if the fault lies within its protected zone:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Standard Three-Zone Stepped Distance Scheme:) Tj
T*
(   - Zone 1: Set to protect 80% to 85% of line length. Operates instantaneously \($t_1 \\approx) Tj
T*
(   15-20$ ms\). It never covers 100% to avoid overreaching into the next bus due to CT/PT) Tj
T*
(   measurement inaccuracies.) Tj
T*
(   - Zone 2: Set to cover 100% of line plus 20-50% of the shortest adjacent line. Operates with a) Tj
T*
(   coordinated time delay \($t_2 \\approx 300-400$ ms\) to allow Zone 1 of adjacent line to trip) Tj
T*
(   first.) Tj
T*
(   - Zone 3: Set to protect 100% of line plus 100% of the longest adjacent line, serving as remote) Tj
T*
(   backup protection with time delay $t_3 \\approx 800-1000$ ms.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
